## Picture-in-Picture (PiP)

### Android
- Use `floating` or native PiP via platform channels.
- Enter PiP when navigating away from player page.
- Show camera name and status in PiP overlay.
- Minimum size: 240x135 (16:9).

### iOS
- Use `AVPictureInPictureController` via platform channel.
- Requires `Background Modes: Audio, AirPlay, and Picture in Picture` capability.
- Only works with a single stream.

### Implementation
```dart
class PipController {
  /// Enter PiP mode if supported
  Future<void> enterPip() async {
    if (await isPipSupported()) {
      await _platform.invokeMethod('enterPip', {
        'aspectRatio': [16, 9],
      });
    }
  }

  /// Check if device supports PiP
  Future<bool> isPipSupported() async {
    return await _platform.invokeMethod<bool>('isPipSupported') ?? false;
  }
}
```

## Background Playback

### Persistent Notification
- When the user leaves the app while streaming, show a persistent notification.
- Notification displays: camera name, status (live), and controls (stop).
- Use a foreground service (Android) to keep the stream alive.

### Audio-Only Mode
- For bandwidth savings, offer audio-only background mode.
- Useful for two-way audio monitoring.

## Reconnection Strategy

### Auto-Reconnect
- On stream interruption, attempt reconnection automatically.
- Backoff schedule: 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s (max 5 attempts).
- Show reconnecting UI state during attempts.
- After max attempts, show error with manual retry button.

```dart
class StreamReconnector {
  static const maxAttempts = 5;

  Future<void> reconnect(String cameraId) async {
    for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {
      try {
        await _connect(cameraId);
        return; // Success
      } catch (_) {
        if (attempt < maxAttempts) {
          await Future.delayed(Duration(seconds: math.pow(2, attempt).toInt()));
        }
      }
    }
    throw const AppException.stream('Nao foi possivel reconectar. Verifique sua conexao.');
  }
}
```

## Resource Management

### Player Lifecycle
- Initialize player only when the widget is visible.
- Pause/stop when widget goes off-screen (e.g., scrolled out of grid).
- Dispose player controller on widget disposal.
- Release system resources (decoders, GPU surfaces) promptly.

### Memory Management
- Limit decoded frame buffer size.
- Monitor memory usage; reduce quality if pressure is high.
- In grid view, only decode visible cells; pause off-screen streams.

### Battery Optimization
- Reduce frame rate when battery is below 20%.
- Offer "low power mode" with 15fps and 480p cap.
- Disable background streaming when battery is below 10%.

## Rules

- NEVER render a video frame before the player is fully initialized and data is available.
- NEVER allow more concurrent streams than the network-based limit.
- NEVER leave a player controller undisposed — always clean up.
- NEVER play streams in background without a foreground service (Android) or background mode (iOS).
- ALWAYS manage player state via Riverpod — never use widget-local state.
- ALWAYS implement auto-reconnection with exponential backoff.
- ALWAYS adapt quality based on current network conditions.
- ALWAYS show clear loading/buffering/error states to the user.
- ALWAYS handle app lifecycle transitions (pause, resume, detach) for players.
- ALWAYS prefer HLS for standard monitoring; reserve WebRTC for critical low-latency needs.
